  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering CSS Variable Changes**\n\nOkay, I\'ve got it. I\'m focusing on the CSS variables. Specifically, what if I redefine `accent-crimson` and `accent-crimson-deep`? What about using Navy Blue and Gold instead? I\'m getting there.\n\n\n**Refining Variable Renaming**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the variable renaming strategy. I\'m oscillating between two options: either keeping the existing names (`--color-accent-crimson`) and just changing their values to blue, or, renaming the variables entirely to reflect blue colors. The former seems simpler initially, but the latter is more maintainable long-term. Replacing is easy, and I\'m leaning toward the latter.\n\n\n**Analyzing Color Brand Alignment**\n\nI\'ve just been reviewing `index.css` and am seeing the potential for a smoother transition to the new color scheme. Redefining the crimson variables to represent the actual brand color is an option. I\'m now leaning toward using more descriptive variable names that reflect the Cars on Caledon brand: `brand-primary` for the Royal Blue and `brand-secondary` for the Yellow/Gold.\n\n\n\n**Implementing Global Replacements**\n\nI\'m now thinking about the actual implementation. My initial idea of manually changing variables seems clunky, and I\'ve pivoted toward a global find-and-replace strategy. I\'ve realized that using a script with `sed` or Node.js to replace strings in the codebase is incredibly fast, precise, and avoids the issues of target content not being found. I\'m excited about exploring this approach!\n\n\n**Defining Brand Color Palettes**\n\nI\'m now determining the exact brand color palette for Cars on Caledon. After reviewing the Facebook page, I\'m confident about using a Royal Blue and Gold pairing. I am also investigating variations of the blue, such as `#004B87` and `#0F2C59`, and also the Gold variants. I\'m focusing on a premium Royal Blue, and the beautiful gold color that complements it.\n\n\n**Confirming CSS Variable Overhaul**\n\nI\'m solidifying the CSS variable overhaul strategy now, and am making some exciting refinements. I\'m going with brand-specific variables, and will replace crimson with Royal Blue (`#0F2C59`, `#1E3A8A`) and deep crimson with a darker blue (`#0A1931`). Gold variants like `#C9A96A` and `#D4AF37` will replace the original highlights. I\'m focusing on making everything high-end and on-brand.\n\n\n**Implementing Color Scheme Changes**\n\nI\'m now implementing the color scheme changes, focusing on `index.css`. I\'m replacing `--color-accent-crimson` with `--color-brand-blue` (Royal/Navy Blue), and `--color-accent-crimson-deep` with `--color-brand-blue-deep` (Deepest Navy). I\'m also ensuring that all existing references to the previous variables are updated. I\'m now testing in Tailwind.\n\n\n**Mapping Color Implementations**\n\nI\'m now integrating the brand colors throughout the UI. Tailwind\'s auto-generated classes for `bg-brand-blue`, `text-brand-blue`, etc. are incredibly efficient. I\'m focusing on replacing all instances of the old color names and am meticulously mapping each usage to the new palette. I am mapping, in particular, instances of `accent-crimson` usages, and am focusing on the Navbar\'s utility bar items, logo, and hover indicators. The `Navbar.tsx` file is where I\'ll do this. I\'ll make the utility bar items gold (`text-brand-gold`), the logo\'s background a gradient of blue, and the \"CC\" letters gold. I am focusing on making the hover indicators `bg-brand-gold`.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 12
